WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.969 --> 00:00:05.759
Stranger Things is a show about kids that
could even be mistaken for a kids' show, but

00:00:05.759 --> 00:00:10.330
grown-ups watching the series will notice
a lot of details that might escape a younger

00:00:10.330 --> 00:00:11.330
eye.

00:00:11.330 --> 00:00:13.440
This is what only adults notice in Stranger
Things.

00:00:14.850 --> 00:00:18.850
At first glance, Hopper's just a likable,
classic tough guy caricature.

00:00:18.850 --> 00:00:23.159
He drinks every night — not just a beer
or two, but drinking to the point where he's

00:00:23.159 --> 00:00:24.550
numb drunk.

00:00:24.550 --> 00:00:29.390
In season one of Stranger Things, his office
knows every morning means, quote, "coffee

00:00:29.390 --> 00:00:30.710
and contemplation."

00:00:30.710 --> 00:00:36.330
Hopper rips cigarettes all day and when he's
not downing a delightful combo of Eggo waffles,

00:00:36.330 --> 00:00:41.230
whipped cream, and candy, he's regularly devouring
plates full of fried meats.

00:00:41.230 --> 00:00:45.990
As a kid, it seems funny how he eats like
a slob, but as an adult you wouldn't be wrong

00:00:45.990 --> 00:00:51.550
to think the man is an alcoholic just a few
terrorist fistfights away from a heart attack.

00:00:51.550 --> 00:00:55.580
Hopper spends basically every day in season
one hungover.

00:00:55.580 --> 00:01:00.500
He doesn't get drunk as much in season 2,
although he has a nightly beer, or three,

00:01:00.500 --> 00:01:01.530
or six.

00:01:01.530 --> 00:01:04.430
He's the kind of man who's likely never eaten
a salad.

00:01:04.430 --> 00:01:08.930
Nor could one imagine him on a treadmill,
though he does have a pretty physical job

00:01:08.930 --> 00:01:13.600
filled with stress and the occasional protracted
fight with a Russian Terminator.

00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:18.530
While in the '80s it was still pretty acceptable
to survive off nothing but greasy meat, that

00:01:18.530 --> 00:01:20.480
wasn't a healthy way to live.

00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:24.130
Throw in Hopper's heavy drinking, and you
have a man who today would be considered on

00:01:24.130 --> 00:01:25.690
the edge of physical collapse.

00:01:27.130 --> 00:01:31.850
Mike and Nancy Wheeler are two of the most
dynamic, interesting young people in Hawkins,

00:01:31.850 --> 00:01:37.760
which makes it astounding that their father
is as much of a detached non-presence as possible.

00:01:37.760 --> 00:01:42.200
He's completely oblivious to the fact that
both his children are wrapped up in a multi-dimensional

00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:44.220
battle against a super-monster.

00:01:44.220 --> 00:01:48.770
So is Mrs. Wheeler, but eventually she seems
to realize that something is up with her kids.

00:01:48.770 --> 00:01:52.600
By season 3, we learn that there's more to
her than meets the eye, and if anything, she's

00:01:52.600 --> 00:01:56.159
simply been dragged down by her narcoleptic
husband.

00:01:56.159 --> 00:02:00.720
Mr. Wheeler, despite all the drama revolving
around them, never notices anything out of

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:01.869
the ordinary.

00:02:01.869 --> 00:02:06.450
Aside from a speech about teamwork he gives
to Mike, he spends most of the time at home

00:02:06.450 --> 00:02:07.450
sleeping.

00:02:07.450 --> 00:02:10.200
Even more, Mr. Wheeler is a dud of a husband.

00:02:10.200 --> 00:02:14.920
At the end of season 2, we see Mrs. Wheeler's
jaw drop when Billy stops by while her husband

00:02:14.920 --> 00:02:17.230
is passed out in his easy chair.

00:02:17.230 --> 00:02:22.070
By season 3, we realize how unfulfilled Mrs.
Wheeler is in her marriage when she makes

00:02:22.070 --> 00:02:26.400
plans to meet Billy at a motel, even though
she doesn't act on them.

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:31.320
Mr. Wheeler is an inattentive father and husband,
but in reality, it's tragic that he's surrounded

00:02:31.320 --> 00:02:34.900
by people so full of life while he densely
snores away.

00:02:36.020 --> 00:02:40.700
Yes, these kids are nerds, choosing to hang
out in the Hawkins AV Club instead of the

00:02:40.700 --> 00:02:45.569
football field, with basically no friends
outside their group except their science teacher.

00:02:45.569 --> 00:02:49.209
In fact they may be the whole of the Hawkins
AV club.

00:02:49.209 --> 00:02:54.130
For their public school to have a ham radio
and an AV club is certainly out of the ordinary.

00:02:54.130 --> 00:02:57.540
But nerds or not, they have some pretty expensive
hardware.

00:02:57.540 --> 00:03:05.430
Their radios, the Radio Shack TRC-206, TRC-214,
and TRC-219, weren't cheap.

00:03:05.430 --> 00:03:10.349
Plus Dustin has a headset and later builds
Cerebro — a portable super-powerful radio

00:03:10.349 --> 00:03:11.930
— at science camp.

00:03:11.930 --> 00:03:15.450
The boys certainly have free rein to play
with expensive toys.

00:03:15.450 --> 00:03:19.130
While their parents were likely more than
happy to encourage their scientific endeavors,

00:03:19.130 --> 00:03:23.840
the walkies and hundreds of batteries they
must go through in a year would make a pretty

00:03:23.840 --> 00:03:25.310
expensive outlay.

00:03:25.310 --> 00:03:28.450
And this is before everything had USB charging
cables.

00:03:29.450 --> 00:03:33.000
Murray Bauman is the consummate matchmaker
in Stranger Things.

00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:37.590
When Jonathan Byers and Nancy Wheeler come
to him in season 2 for help, the two teenagers

00:03:37.590 --> 00:03:41.200
have a bit too much vodka and wind up sleeping
over.

00:03:41.200 --> 00:03:45.080
Bauman is surprised they're not together and
says they should just have sex.

00:03:45.080 --> 00:03:48.910
They protest, and Jonathan volunteers to sleep
on the pullout.

00:03:48.910 --> 00:03:53.471
That night, the teenagers can't sleep, and
when neither can hold out any longer, they

00:03:53.471 --> 00:03:55.590
take their friendship to the next level.

00:03:55.590 --> 00:03:59.349
The next morning at breakfast, Bauman asks
with a wry smile,

00:03:59.349 --> 00:04:01.409
"How was the pullout?"

00:04:01.409 --> 00:04:05.310
A kid watching will think he's just joking
about the fact that Jonathan didn't sleep

00:04:05.310 --> 00:04:06.940
on the pullout sofa bed.

00:04:06.940 --> 00:04:12.100
A grownup, however, will see the double entendre
he makes with the pullout sofa and a particular

00:04:12.100 --> 00:04:13.709
move that happens in the bedroom.

00:04:14.709 --> 00:04:18.989
During interrogation, the Russians give Steve
and Robin a truth serum.

00:04:18.989 --> 00:04:23.830
The drug could be anything, but based on the
reaction, at least in a grown-up's eye, it's

00:04:23.830 --> 00:04:25.720
likely a psychoactive drug.

00:04:25.720 --> 00:04:30.740
It's worth mentioning that MKUltra, the program
that gave rise to Eleven's psychic powers,

00:04:30.740 --> 00:04:35.330
was known in the real world to experiment
with hallucinogenics and psychology primarily

00:04:35.330 --> 00:04:37.400
for interrogation purposes.

00:04:37.400 --> 00:04:42.190
When Steve and Robin start laughing and joking
with each other, to kids they seem drunk;

00:04:42.190 --> 00:04:44.440
but adults will understand that they're tripping.

00:04:44.440 --> 00:04:45.710
"They seem drunk."

00:04:45.710 --> 00:04:47.840
"Why would they be drunk?"

00:04:47.840 --> 00:04:52.871
After having a mind-blowing experience watching
Back to the Future on hallucinogens, Steve

00:04:52.871 --> 00:04:57.650
lists it as one of his favorite movies when
he interviews to work at a video rental store.

00:04:57.650 --> 00:05:01.150
While sitting outside the theater, the lights
on the ceiling of the mall begin to send off

00:05:01.150 --> 00:05:03.150
chasers for Robin and Steve.

00:05:03.150 --> 00:05:07.360
That lands them in the bathroom, throwing
up and then having one of the greatest heart-to-hearts

00:05:07.360 --> 00:05:09.220
in Stranger Things history.

00:05:09.220 --> 00:05:13.080
Considering that these two were dosed with
some strong psychoactive drugs, Steve's quick

00:05:13.080 --> 00:05:16.139
recovery and ability to drive a car are pretty
astounding.

00:05:17.229 --> 00:05:21.660
There is no way to explain how it was having
only one phone in the house.

00:05:21.660 --> 00:05:26.800
Younger people will never understand the necessity
of taking a phone call standing in the kitchen,

00:05:26.800 --> 00:05:31.330
in front of anyone who wants to listen, because
the phone was mounted on the wall next to

00:05:31.330 --> 00:05:32.330
the kitchen.

00:05:32.330 --> 00:05:36.210
For the grown-ups who mostly remember it,
the central shared phone brings back conflicted

00:05:36.210 --> 00:05:37.210
memories.

00:05:37.210 --> 00:05:41.100
Yes, there was something nice about not always
being accessible.

00:05:41.100 --> 00:05:43.780
But it hindered mobility and privacy.

00:05:43.780 --> 00:05:47.520
Sometimes that short cord was replaced by
a long one you could stretch into any room

00:05:47.520 --> 00:05:51.520
in the house, though it was easy to figure
out when it was a private call.

00:05:51.520 --> 00:05:55.189
And even if you were in another room, you
could never guarantee the call was private

00:05:55.189 --> 00:06:00.020
— like when Mrs. Wheeler picks up the other
phone to listen in on Mike's call with El,

00:06:00.020 --> 00:06:02.410
only to hear him lie about his grandmother
being ill.

00:06:03.530 --> 00:06:08.640
As an adult in the era of playdates, constant
chauffeuring, and stranger danger, it can

00:06:08.640 --> 00:06:13.389
be shocking to recall a time when parents
let their kids go off on their own all day.

00:06:13.389 --> 00:06:17.750
Yet, the kids of Stranger Things bike around
by themselves from house to house, over what

00:06:17.750 --> 00:06:20.000
is likely miles of empty road.

00:06:20.000 --> 00:06:25.650
In season 3 of Stranger Things, Lucas's 10-year-old
sister, Erica, is left completely unsupervised

00:06:25.650 --> 00:06:26.699
at the mall.

00:06:26.699 --> 00:06:31.900
Her parents don't even seem to notice that
she spends basically a day in a Russian bunker.

00:06:31.900 --> 00:06:34.560
In the '80s, kids were mostly left to their
own devices.

00:06:34.560 --> 00:06:38.479
They made friends on their own, biked home
by themselves, and their parents let them

00:06:38.479 --> 00:06:40.639
take care of themselves most of the day.

00:06:40.639 --> 00:06:45.100
Sure, that lack of supervision allowed Will
to be snatched by the Demogorgon , leaving

00:06:45.100 --> 00:06:47.610
his brother in charge of surveillance duty.

00:06:47.610 --> 00:06:52.130
But it also empowered the kids to figure out
what was going on and save him, and eventually

00:06:52.130 --> 00:06:53.690
the town, without parental intrusion.

00:06:54.690 --> 00:06:59.240
The old world was very disconnected compared
to our present world, which adds a lot to

00:06:59.240 --> 00:07:01.199
the story in Stranger Things.

00:07:01.199 --> 00:07:04.860
Only grown-ups will fully notice and remember
how disconnected it was.

00:07:04.860 --> 00:07:09.450
After all, how many times is everybody separated
and looking for each other in this show?

00:07:09.450 --> 00:07:13.770
If they had cell phones, they could easily
call each other to figure out where everyone

00:07:13.770 --> 00:07:15.080
else is located.

00:07:15.080 --> 00:07:19.310
Yes, the kids do that with their walkies,
but as we see in season 2, they're not always

00:07:19.310 --> 00:07:21.440
equipped with these handy devices.

00:07:21.440 --> 00:07:25.250
In the modern connected world, however, people
are constantly cell-phone accessible.

00:07:25.250 --> 00:07:29.789
Today, many parents have GPS trackers on their
kids' cell phones, but in this series, there

00:07:29.789 --> 00:07:32.500
is no easy way to figure out where the children
are.

00:07:32.500 --> 00:07:36.830
In season one, we see how much time Hopper
and company spend researching to find out

00:07:36.830 --> 00:07:39.940
that HNL was involved with MKUltra.

00:07:39.940 --> 00:07:45.050
In season 3, when the group doesn't know Planck's
Constant, they have to get Dustin to use his

00:07:45.050 --> 00:07:50.479
super-powerful radio to connect to his brainiac
girlfriend, Susie, in Salt Lake City because

00:07:50.479 --> 00:07:54.810
she is the only person they know who understands
the mathematical formulation.

00:07:54.810 --> 00:07:58.860
With the Internet, however, you can look up
Planck's Constant in half a second.

00:07:58.860 --> 00:08:04.099
"Planck's Constant is 6.62607004"

00:08:04.099 --> 00:08:08.910
In season 3 of Stranger Things, we see the
impact the Starcourt Mall has had on the small

00:08:08.910 --> 00:08:10.949
town of Hawkins, Indiana.

00:08:10.949 --> 00:08:14.860
Before malls, people walked through downtown
areas, going to random shops and — in bigger

00:08:14.860 --> 00:08:16.500
cities — department stores.

00:08:16.500 --> 00:08:21.660
But shopping was mostly about independent
shop owners and locally-owned stores, or sometimes

00:08:21.660 --> 00:08:22.970
mail order.

00:08:22.970 --> 00:08:27.350
Then malls brought in chains owned by far-off
conglomerates, and while everybody protested

00:08:27.350 --> 00:08:32.110
the death of downtown districts, the malls
became a central point of American culture.

00:08:32.110 --> 00:08:36.089
They started in big cities but quickly spread
to smaller towns.

00:08:36.089 --> 00:08:39.899
Many adults have memories of the early days
when they would go to the mall and spend a

00:08:39.899 --> 00:08:44.169
few hours walking around, shopping, catching
a movie, and eating at the food court.

00:08:44.169 --> 00:08:48.290
This type of shopping center dominated for
a few decades until the internet came around.

00:08:48.290 --> 00:08:52.170
Now, malls are lamenting their demise at the
hands of e-retail.

00:08:52.170 --> 00:08:57.720
Ironically, buying local in-store or online
at downtown shops seems to have made a great

00:08:57.720 --> 00:08:59.029
resurgence.

00:08:59.029 --> 00:09:03.319
Kids today may never understand how, for a
brief while, malls were king.

00:09:04.319 --> 00:09:08.790
In season 2 of Stranger Things, Steve and
Nancy go to the home of Barb's parents to

00:09:08.790 --> 00:09:10.720
talk about her disappearance.

00:09:10.720 --> 00:09:14.259
The parents couldn't get it together to cook
so they ordered KFC for dinner.

00:09:14.259 --> 00:09:19.339
Joe Keery, who plays Steve Harrington, spouts
ad-worthy lines about the finger-lickin' good

00:09:19.339 --> 00:09:24.420
chicken, and adults who grew up with overt
product placements would likely take notice.

00:09:24.420 --> 00:09:28.920
Brands in storylines were a constant since
TV evolved into a machine driven solely by

00:09:28.920 --> 00:09:29.920
ad revenue.

00:09:29.920 --> 00:09:34.290
They were eventually replaced by more expensive
commercials, followed by the service subscription

00:09:34.290 --> 00:09:35.290
model.

00:09:35.290 --> 00:09:37.680
And another product placement an adult might
notice?

00:09:37.680 --> 00:09:38.829
New Coke.

00:09:38.829 --> 00:09:43.220
Lucas sounds like a commercial as he pontificates
to his friends on the quality of this soda.

00:09:43.220 --> 00:09:48.410
In 1985, New Coke was an attempt to revitalize
the brand and Lucas's description sounds like

00:09:48.410 --> 00:09:49.999
perfect advertising.

00:09:49.999 --> 00:09:53.690
Coca-Cola even reissued "New Coke" as a tie-in
to the show.

00:09:53.690 --> 00:09:57.949
In that regard, Stranger Things has used an
old marketing trick in a tongue-in-cheek way

00:09:57.949 --> 00:10:01.920
to bring old-world marketing to modern television
without pricey commercials.

00:10:03.029 --> 00:10:07.220
California may have lost some of its luster,
given the growth of other cities and the high

00:10:07.220 --> 00:10:11.000
cost of living, but in the '80s, it was the
place to be.

00:10:11.000 --> 00:10:15.069
While Chicago enjoyed popularity thanks to
John Hughes, and New York has always had a

00:10:15.069 --> 00:10:19.279
strong presence, many movies and TV shows
about adolescence in the '80s were set in

00:10:19.279 --> 00:10:20.399
California.

00:10:20.399 --> 00:10:24.769
In many ways, the trends found in California
trickled to the rest of the country, as evidenced

00:10:24.769 --> 00:10:28.129
by Lucas and Dustin using popular phrases
around Max.

00:10:28.129 --> 00:10:33.350
"You're super smart, and you're like 'totally
tubular.'"

00:10:33.350 --> 00:10:38.399
This was the era before social media and online
shopping, when the only way to know the trends

00:10:38.399 --> 00:10:40.949
was to be in the epicenter.

00:10:40.949 --> 00:10:45.100
So to leave that world would almost be like
falling back in time.

00:10:45.100 --> 00:10:48.939
Small towns could be a season, a year, or
more behind the trends.

00:10:48.939 --> 00:10:53.540
Billy is infuriated by this, though his status
as a guy from California gives him instant

00:10:53.540 --> 00:10:55.339
cachet in Hawkins.

00:10:55.339 --> 00:11:00.369
With that said, leaving California for someplace
else would have hit harder in 1984 than almost

00:11:00.369 --> 00:11:02.160
any other time in history.

00:11:02.160 --> 00:11:05.790
Because of this, a person can almost understand
Billy's frustration.

